Pouch dup and the fcntl descriptor commands over native 129-131 (B-3b, vote 7).

dup(fd) is SYS_thyla_handle_dup_to(fd, 0, LOWEST): the lowest free slot,
picked and filled in one kernel step (it was __NR_dup 0xFFFF -> ENOSYS, which
failed every file load in libxml2, whose xmlInputFromFd dup()s each fd).
fcntl gains F_DUPFD / F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC (the same call with the floor as x1; a
negative floor widens to a huge one, which the kernel answers EINVAL after a
closed fd's EBADF, Linux's order), F_GETFL (the status word FLAGS_GET reports:
access mode, O_NONBLOCK, O_APPEND; OEXEC reads as O_RDONLY) and F_SETFL
(O_NONBLOCK through native SYS_SET_NONBLOCK 123; restating O_APPEND is
accepted, flipping it is EINVAL because the append lives in the server's open
of the file; O_ASYNC / O_DIRECT / O_NOATIME are EINVAL; every other bit is
ignored, as Linux ignores it). pipe2 now honours O_CLOEXEC (FLAGS_SET) and
O_NONBLOCK (123) on both ends, set after the pipe exists, closing both if a
set fails (it accepted and ignored both). fdopendir's `fcntl(fd, F_GETFL) & O_PATH` read
-1 & O_PATH as set and answered EBADF for every directory; it now works.
Tagged Pouch sockets keep ENOSYS here (B-3k gives them kernel descriptors).

